== Great Effects on the Body

II. It is no sign that affections have the nature of true religion, or that they do not, that they have great effects on the body.

All affections whatever have, in some respect or degree, an effect on the body. As was observed before, such is our nature, and such are the laws of the union of soul and body, that the mind can have no lively or vigorous exercise without some effect on the body. So subject is the body to the mind, and so closely do its fluids, especially the animal spirits, follow the motions and exercises of the mind, that there cannot be so much as an intense thought without an effect on them. Indeed, it is questionable whether a soul in the body ever so much as thinks one thought, or has any exercise at all, without some corresponding motion, or change of motion, in some degree, of the fluids in some part of the body. But universal experience shows that the exercise of the affections has, in a special way, a tendency to some perceptible effect on the body. And if it is so, that all affections have some effect on the body, we may well suppose that the greater those affections are, and the more vigorous their exercise (other circumstances being equal), the greater the effect on the body will be. So it is no wonder that very #emph[great] and strong exercises of the affections should have #emph[great] effects on the body. And therefore, seeing there are very great affections, both common and spiritual, it is no wonder that great effects on the body should arise from both these kinds of affections. And consequently these effects are no signs that the affections they arise from are of one kind or the other.

Great effects on the body are certainly no sure evidence that affections are spiritual. For we see that such effects often arise from great affections about temporal things, when religion has no part in them at all. And if great affections about worldly things, which are purely natural, may have these effects, I do not know by what rule we should decide that high affections about religious things, which arise in the same way from nature, cannot have the same effect.

Nor, on the other hand, do I know of any rule anyone has to decide that gracious and holy affections, when raised as high as any natural affections, and having equally strong and vigorous exercises, cannot have a great effect on the body. No such rule can be drawn from reason. I know of no reason why being affected with a view of God’s glory should not cause the body to faint, as well as being affected with a view of #emph[Solomon’s] glory. And no such rule has yet been produced from the Scripture. None has ever been found in all the recent controversies there have been about things of this nature. There is a great power in spiritual affections. We read of the power that works in Christians,#footnote[Eph 3:7.] and of the Spirit of God being in them as the Spirit of power,#footnote[2 Tim 1:7.] and of the effectual working of his power in them,#footnote[Ibid. v. 7. Ibid. v. 19.] indeed, of the working of God’s mighty power in them.#footnote[Eph 1:19.] But man’s nature is weak. Flesh and blood are represented in Scripture as exceedingly weak, and particularly in their unfitness for great spiritual and heavenly operations and exercises (Matt 26:41; 1 Cor 15:43, 50). The text we are considering speaks of #emph[joy unspeakable and full of glory.] And who that considers what man’s nature is, and what the nature of the affections is, can reasonably doubt that such unutterable and glorious joys may be too great and mighty for weak dust and ashes, so as to overpower it considerably? It is evident from the Scripture that true divine discoveries, or ideas of God’s glory, when given in a great degree, have a tendency, by affecting the mind, to overpower the body. For the Scripture often teaches us that if these ideas or views were given to the degree in which they are given in heaven, the weak frame of the body could not survive under them, and that no man can see God in that way and live. The knowledge the saints have of God’s beauty and glory in this world, and the holy affections that arise from it, are of the same nature and kind as what the saints experience in heaven, differing only in degree and circumstances. What God gives them here is a foretaste of heavenly happiness and an earnest of their future inheritance. And who will limit God in his giving of this earnest, or say that he may give so much of the inheritance, such a part of the future reward, as an earnest of the whole, and no more? And seeing God has taught us in his word that the whole reward is such that it would at once destroy the body, is it not too bold a thing for us to set bounds to the sovereign God in this way? Is it not too bold to say that in giving the earnest of this reward in this world, he will never give so much of it as to lessen the strength of the body in the least, when God has nowhere limited himself like this?

The psalmist, speaking of vehement religious affections he had, speaks of an effect in his flesh or body besides what was in his soul, expressly distinguishing the one from the other, again and again: “My soul longs, even faints, for the courts of the LORD; my #emph[heart] and my #emph[flesh] cry out for the living God” (Ps 84:2). Here is a plain distinction between the heart and the flesh, as each being affected. So in Ps 63:1: “My #emph[soul] thirsts for You. My #emph[flesh] yearns for You in a dry and weary land without water.” Here also is an evident, intended distinction between the soul and the flesh.

The prophet #emph[Habakkuk] speaks of his body being overpowered by a sense of the majesty of God: “I heard, and my body trembled; my lips quivered at the sound. Decay entered my bones; I trembled where I stood” (Hab 3:16). So the psalmist speaks expressly of his flesh trembling: “My flesh trembles in awe of You” (Ps 119:120).

That such ideas of God’s glory as are sometimes given in this world have a tendency to overpower the body is evident, because the Scripture tells us that this has at times actually been the effect of the external manifestations God has made of himself to some of the saints. These were made for this very end, #emph[namely,] to give them an idea of God’s majesty and glory. We have such instances in the prophet #emph[Daniel] and the apostle #emph[John. Daniel,] giving an account of an external representation of the glory of Christ, says, “No strength remained in me; my face grew deathly pale, and I was powerless” (Dan 10:8). And the apostle #emph[John,] giving an account of a similar manifestation made to him, says, “When I saw Him, I fell at His feet like a dead man” (Rev 1:17). It is useless to say here that what these saints saw were only external manifestations or symbols of the glory of Christ. It is true that they were outward representations of Christ’s glory, which they saw with their bodily eyes. Yet the end and use of these external symbols or representations was to give these prophets an idea of the thing represented. And that was the true divine glory and majesty of Christ, which is his spiritual glory. They were used only as signs of this spiritual glory, and this is undoubtedly how the prophets received them, made use of them, and were affected by them. According to the end for which God intended these outward signs, they received by them a great and lively apprehension of the real glory and majesty of God’s nature, of which they were signs. And so they were greatly affected, their souls swallowed up and their bodies overpowered. And I think those are very bold and daring who will say that God cannot, or will not, give the same clear and affecting ideas and apprehensions of the same real glory and majesty of his nature to any of his saints without the intervention of any such external shadows of it.

Before I leave this head, I would further observe that the Scripture plainly often uses bodily effects to express the strength of holy and spiritual affections, such as #emph[trembling,]#footnote[Ps 119:120; Ezra 9:4; Isa 66:2, 5; Hab 3:16.] #emph[groaning,]#footnote[Rom 8:26.] #emph[being sick,]#footnote[#emph[Song 2:5; 5:8.]] #emph[crying out,]#footnote[Ps 84:2.] #emph[panting,]#footnote[Ps 38:10; 42:1; 119:131.] and #emph[fainting.]#footnote[Ps 84:2; 119:81.] Now suppose these are only figurative expressions, to represent the degree of affection. Yet I hope all will allow that they are fit and suitable figures to represent the high degree of those spiritual affections which the Spirit of God uses them to represent. And I do not see how they would be, if those spiritual affections, however high their degree, have no tendency to any such things, but if, on the contrary, these things are the proper effects and sad tokens of false affections and of the delusion of the devil. I cannot think God would commonly use things which are very foreign to spiritual affections, and are strong marks of the hand of #emph[Satan,] and smell strongly of the bottomless pit, as beautiful figures to represent the high degree of holy and heavenly affections.
